WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.629
Stereoscopic vision means seeing with two eyes that face forward, a left eye and a right eye. The eyes are a few centimetres apart. So each eye sees the ball from a slightly different angle.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:26.384
So the left eye's view and the right eye's view are a little different. The brain combines the two views into one three-dimensional, or 3D, image. 3D means we see depth: how far away things are.

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:36.268
Steps one and two: say there are two eyes, each giving a slightly different view. Then say the brain combines the views into a 3D image.

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:52.163
Step three: link it to the task. When the ball is near, the two views are very different. When it is far away, the difference is small. So the brain can judge distance, and how quickly it changes, which is speed. Judging is the key.

00:00:52.750 --> 00:01:04.867
A person who can see with only one eye has no stereoscopic vision. They can still see the ball. But with only one view, the brain has nothing to compare, so distance is harder to judge.

00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:22.175
Step four, for an investigation: a fair test. Change only one thing: catching with both eyes, then with one eye covered. Keep the same person, ball, throwing distance and light. Repeat, for example ten throws each, with many people. Count the catches.

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:36.143
Now a real exam question. It asks you to explain why a person using both eyes finds it easier to catch a ball than a person who is blind in one eye. Three marks, so give three ideas.

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:45.532
Idea one: the person using both eyes has stereoscopic, or 3D, vision. The person who is blind in one eye does not.

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:54.804
Idea two: why? Each eye sees the ball from a slightly different angle, so the two views are slightly different. The brain combines them.

00:01:55.400 --> 00:02:04.381
Idea three: so the brain judges the distance and speed of the ball more accurately. The person can put their hands in the right place at the right time.

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:10.475
Here is the full answer. This answer is by olevels.com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:26.064
A common mistake, often in multiple choice, is to link stereoscopic vision with colour or focusing. Cells called cones see colour, and the lens focuses. Stereoscopic vision is about depth and distance.

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:37.167
Another mistake is to write: two eyes see more. That misses the key idea. Say each eye gives a slightly different view, and the brain uses them to judge distance.

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:49.078
In an investigation, do not test different people with one eye and with two eyes. Some people are simply better at catching. Test the same people both ways, and say what you measure.

00:02:50.200 --> 00:03:08.419
Your turn. Ravi wants to find out if people can put a lid on a pen more easily with two eyes than with one. Part a: describe how he could do this investigation. Part b: Ravi says that with one eye, you cannot see the pen. Explain why he is wrong. Pause here and work it out.

00:03:09.550 --> 00:03:21.945
Part a. Use the same people. Each person holds a pen at arm's length and tries to put the lid on, with both eyes, then with one eye covered. Keep the pen, the distance and the light the same.

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:32.100
Give ten tries each way, and test many people. Count how many times out of ten the lid goes on. Compare the totals for both eyes and for one eye.

00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:45.322
Part b. With only one eye, Ravi can still see the pen. But there is only one view, so there is no stereoscopic vision. The brain cannot judge the distance to the pen well, so the lid misses.
